## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a compact, eviction-aware data structure for time-windowed counting, a common pattern in real-time analytics.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window eviction via timestamped events
- Bucketed counts: map from count to set of users for fast top-k
- Invariants: update counts atomically during insertion and eviction
- Complexity: O(log n) updates, O(k log n) top-k retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowCounter {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.count = new Map(); // user -> cnt
    this.buckets = new Map(); // cnt -> Set<user>
    this.events = new Deque(); // (ts, user)
  }
  _addToBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) this.buckets.set(cnt, new Set());
    this.buckets.get(cnt).add(user);
  }
  _removeFromBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) return;
    this.buckets.get(cnt).delete(user);
    if (this.buckets.get(cnt).size === 0) this.buckets.delete(cnt);
  }
  prune(now) {
    while (this.events.length && this.events.peek().ts <= now - this.W) {
      const {ts, user} = this.events.shift();
      const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
      if (c > 0) {
        this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
        const nc = c - 1;
        if (nc > 0) {
          this._addToBucket(nc, user);
          this.count.set(user, nc);
        } else {
          this.count.delete(user);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  addEvent(user, ts) {
    this.events.push({ts, user});
    this.prune(ts);
    const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
    if (c > 0) this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
    const nc = c + 1;
    this._addToBucket(nc, user);
    this.count.set(user, nc);
  }
  topK(now, k) {
    const res = [];
    // iterate counts from high to low
    const counts = Array.from(this.buckets.keys()).sort((a,b)=>b-a);
    for (const cnt of counts) {
      const set = this.buckets.get(cnt);
      const users = Array.from(set).sort((a,b)=>a-b); // tie-breaker by userId
      for (const u of users) {
        res.push([u, cnt]);
        if (res.length === k) return res;
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support multiple simultaneous windows (e.g., W1 and W2) efficiently?
- How would you handle very large userId spaces with memory constraints, or concurrent updates in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in fully dynamic Li Chao trees, offline lifetime decomposition, and practical handling of duplicates in production settings.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree over time** for lifetime interval decomposition
- **Per-node Li Chao trees** for minimum query aggregation
- **Path-query traversal** across O(log N) nodes
- Memory-time trade-offs for handling large N and duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class LiChao {
  insert(line) { /* insert line into this node's Li Chao tree */ }
  query(x) { /* return minimum y at x across stored lines */ }
}

class DynamicLineSet {
  constructor(N) {
    this.segmentTree = new SegmentTree(N);
  }

  addLine(m, b, id, insertTime) {
    const interval = [insertTime, Infinity];
    this.segmentTree.addInterval(interval, { m, b, id });
  }

  removeLine(id, removeTime) {
    // Update the line's interval end time in the segment tree
    this.segmentTree.closeInterval(id, removeTime);
  }

  queryMin(x, queryTime) {
    return this.segmentTree.queryPath(queryTime)
      .reduce((min, lichao) => Math.min(min, lichao.query(x)), Infinity);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| addLine | O(log N) | O(log N) per line |
| removeLine | O(log N) | - |
| queryMin | O(log² N) | - |

## Edge Cases
- **Identical slopes**: Stored separately with unique ids; Li Chao tree handles min correctly
- **Immediate removal**: Assigned to leaf node only
- **Large N**: Memory scales with O(N log N) total line-node assignments

